WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.090
Welcome, everybody to the second part of this
morning's meeting.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:26.789
We are moving on now to item nine on the agenda,
Digital Health Services by 2020 and Tim, please,

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:28.590
we are very interested to hear from you on
this.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.050
Tim Kelsey: Well, actually, it is interesting.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.320
So, following on from the conversation we
just had, Caroline and I went to a meeting

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.230
with the [inaudible] and various others to
talk about the importance of this.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:44.110
Actually, I think one of the core roles, nationally,
we have the opportunity of doing is setting

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:48.510
standards for, in a way, letting local autonomy
be real.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:56.420
So this paper is essentially proposing, as
part of a very substantial piece of work that's

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.600
being overseen by the National Information
Board that NHS England confirm its commitment

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.000
to the development of a core set of digital
standards that need to become part of the

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.420
basic licence to operate, as it were, for
people, or organisations, that wish to receive

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.640
and treat NHS patients.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:22.130
The benefits case for interoperability or
safe digital record keeping is now overwhelming,

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:28.410
I think, and as a result we, of course, have
many places, many organisations in this country,

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.659
that are setting global standards in interoperability.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:38.020
We have fantastic examples of that at University
Hospitals Birmingham, in Oxford but also in

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:44.310
some of our District General Hospitals; Northampton,
for example, has just developed and implemented

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:49.329
a real time record keeping system on its wards
so that, in the middle of the night, doctors

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:56.520
are making safe decisions about prescribing
and getting automated reports on nursing observations.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:04.290
On its own, that report has reduced the error
rate, in terms of misdiagnosis, by 300%.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:10.179
I will not go into the details but, essentially,
the evidence case is strong.

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:14.689
We also have some good emerging evidence of
the productivity benefits and we have talked

00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:16.760
a lot about the efficiencies opportunity.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.700
One report, which we have commissioned and
which has not yet been published, suggests

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:27.019
that just doing the basics, i.e. simply having
what you could describe as supply side automation,

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:32.010
which is second nature in your warehouses
if you are Tesco or Asda, could save as much

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.150
as £3 billion a year.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:37.900
I think it not only makes common sense but
I think it is almost self-evident that we

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.209
should be doing that.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:43.870
Instead, the vast majority of our hospitals,
in particular, are operating on fax machines

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.260
quite literally held together by bits of string,
in terms of treasury tags and patient records.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.901
However, this is set against the extraordinary
achievement in primary care, where we now

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:58.750
have 100% digital record keeping and with
that we are able to do some very unprecedented

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.949
things, like offer patients access to their
own records, for example and start the revolution

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:07.989
that we have been discussing, the transformation
that we have been talking about.

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:12.260
I would hope, therefore, that the board would
recognise this as an important moment because

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.159
I think the history of technology and healthcare
that it has operated in a kind of silo from

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:21.589
the mainstream operations of patient services.

00:03:21.589 --> 00:03:28.019
What we are now saying is: by 2018, providers
who do not meet these standards, having not

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:33.229
implemented them in primary, urgent and emergency
care services will not be commissioned to

00:03:33.229 --> 00:03:37.329
provide those service and the same will be
true, by 2020, for all other services.

00:03:37.329 --> 00:03:43.859
These are clearly very ambitious targets but
they are not as ambitious as they might initially

00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:44.859
appear.

00:03:44.859 --> 00:03:48.569
Apart from the fact that, as I say, we have
pockets of excellence already gripping the

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:54.169
digital opportunity, we have also been developing
some programmes - the summary care record,

00:03:54.169 --> 00:03:59.959
for example - where we are seeing very, very
rapid rates of adoption as clinical staff

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:05.510
and management begin to realise the benefits
that these kinds of data sharing arrangements

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:06.510
can bring.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:12.979
So, just to give you an example, currently
GP summary care records are being shared between

00:04:12.979 --> 00:04:19.329
GPs and 111 and A&amp;E in, now, nearly 50% of
the NHS in England and that is a dramatic

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:21.519
increase on the situation last year.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:27.550
To some extent, therefore, it is ambitious
but we are building on a solid base of commitment,

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.270
I think, in the health service.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:35.490
What we are saying, in terms of the timetable,
is that we will hope to develop these standards

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:40.360
in collaboration, not just with provider colleagues
with other organisations, such as the NHS

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.580
Confederation and the Foundation Trust Network
and develop the kind of commissioning and

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.660
regulatory environment that will support their
delivery.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.841
However, we will also work with patients and
community organisations just to start, in

00:04:53.841 --> 00:05:00.470
a way, articulating better the benefits case
for implementation of these kinds of standards.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:09.810
By June, we hope to publish guidance to the
health and care system on what the standards

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:14.639
should be and how to go about implementing
them, that is the commitment the National

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:15.639
Information Board has made.

00:05:15.639 --> 00:05:19.990
And we are proposing that, by April of 2016,
both individual organisations, provider organisations

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:28.240
and local area commissioners will publish
roadmaps for attaining those 2018/2020 objectives

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.400
and they will be supportively held to account
for delivery of their own milestones through

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.490
those roadmaps through a process of, kind
of, audits and we have called that the Digital

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:39.120
Maturity Index, described in the paper.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.509
This will, therefore, be a transparent reporting
and self-assessment of the degree to which

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:47.690
local health economies are achieving these
objectives over the next few years.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:54.810
So, in closing, I would just not underestimate
the challenge of achieving these standards

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:55.810
and their implementation.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:59.990
However, I would say that I think we - particularly
given our history, over the last decade of

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:08.830
not getting this right - have adopted an approach
which I hope will be embraced by local frontline

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:14.060
services because what we are basically proposing
is a small but core set of standards, the

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:18.780
NHS number, which need to be implemented but
leaving to them the decisions about how those

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.889
standards are implemented, with whom they
work, which IT companies they might work with

00:06:22.889 --> 00:06:24.199
and so on.

00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:28.770
We want there to be a range and a diversity
of different approaches; we think that's what

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.410
people are asking us to give them.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.050
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Tim.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.560
Let me invite comments.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:36.560
Noel.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:41.499
Noel Gordon: Briefly, Tim, we heard earlier
from Ian on the new models of care, of which

00:06:41.499 --> 00:06:50.270
PACS, in particular, require interoperability
as a critical enabler to make them work.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:58.240
Are we aligned sufficiently on the timeline
between your NIB universal initiative in introducing

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:02.620
interoperability and what our new models of
care will need?

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:09.511
And if there is misalignment, in any sense,
for good reason, how can we help the vanguard

00:07:09.511 --> 00:07:12.620
sites in particular, in an accelerated way?

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:17.219
Tim Kelsey: Ian and I have been talking and
I have been talking to Sam and various other

00:07:17.219 --> 00:07:21.360
colleagues about this very, very intensely
over the last few weeks because it is very

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:22.360
clear to all of us.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:27.699
Indeed, the vanguards are asking for support
to make real not just these kinds of interoperability

00:07:27.699 --> 00:07:31.969
objectives but also other aspects of the digital
opportunity, the data opportunity, that they

00:07:31.969 --> 00:07:34.650
want to embed in their approach to new models
of care.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:38.999
We will therefore need to provide a sort of
package of support, which we have not yet,

00:07:38.999 --> 00:07:44.749
of course, decided with them, to secure exactly
that objective.

00:07:44.749 --> 00:07:51.129
And for that reason, Simon has also asked
if we can embed in all the kinds of initiatives

00:07:51.129 --> 00:08:00.060
that we are incentivising over the next period
of time, a set of core, basic requirements

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.860
around adoption of digital standards.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:10.360
So, for example, we would expect any local
health economy or provider that is receiving

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:16.400
funds from us for transformation to use the
NHS number 100% of the time; you know, basic

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.320
stuff.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.400
We are just discussing at the moment what
those five, high impact, digital requirements

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:28.300
should be; almost preconditions for being
in the family of transformation that we are

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:29.300
working with.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.990
So that is something that we are discussing
at the moment but your point is extremely

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:37.169
well taken and certainly we will need to support
the vanguards and other innovators to get

00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:39.149
to the point of interoperability faster than
we would expect, necessarily, everyone else

00:08:39.149 --> 00:08:40.149
to do it.

00:08:40.149 --> 00:08:41.149
Malcolm Grant: Margaret?

00:08:41.149 --> 00:08:51.660
Margaret Casely Hayford: In a way this goes
back to the question that I asked you and

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.100
Karen earlier on but it is sort of building
on that.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:09:03.130
It is about the other standards, if I can
call them that, the standards for meeting

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:11.889
our obligations with regard to ensuring patient
security, patient privacy and also system

00:09:11.889 --> 00:09:14.070
security and so on.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:22.131
With regard to that, I think what I can see
is, if I was in charge of a CCG, for example,

00:09:22.131 --> 00:09:31.740
or if I was a GP, I would want to own all
of the data and keep everything because it

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:39.140
makes one have greater control over output
and the way in which you are interacting with

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:40.140
patients.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:46.800
However, it strikes me that the metadata and
the ability to respond to requests that might

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:58.930
come from patients would be assisted if the
CSUs were able to support, in the way that

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:09.880
would mean that they had a greater control
over systems and formats and so on.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:17.740
I am wondering whether there is anything that
we should do to encourage that cooperation,

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.050
or reliance on CSUs.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:27.040
Tim Kelsey: So I think there is one really
important principle which we just need to

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.730
kind of get around, which is that what the
National Information Board, NHS England and

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:37.680
Five Year Forward View has said, implicitly,
is that, from now on, any confusion about

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.940
whose data this is, is resolved: this is the
patient's data, the GP, the doctor or the

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:51.750
nurse has the privilege of, in some cases,
acting as a custodian for that data but it

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:52.860
is the patient's data.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:57.600
And what we have said, in the National Information
Board's framework is that, by 2020, we want

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:03.959
people to have the complete control over who
it is that accesses their data, so we want

00:11:03.959 --> 00:11:06.880
to move to a consent based system.

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:11.090
In the meantime, we have to work out how we
maximise the advantage to patients and citizens

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:15.430
of that data being used to the maximum beneficial
clinical effect.

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:19.300
I think there are lots of ways in which we
can support local health economies, particularly

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:24.329
in the vanguards, to start deriving real value
at the bedside, in terms of greater interoperability,

00:11:24.329 --> 00:11:28.561
which is what I have just said, but also,
if I am getting your meaning right, to start

00:11:28.561 --> 00:11:34.209
looking at this data much more effectively
as a source of insight into a real time and

00:11:34.209 --> 00:11:38.839
rapid improvement in the quality of services
provided, assessing outcomes and so on.

00:11:38.839 --> 00:11:44.190
And I think CSUs absolutely have a role to
play in that, in helping to bring analytic

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.560
and other capabilities to bear.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:52.130
On the security issue, which we discussed
just briefly before, it is obviously core

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:56.430
that we cannot trade privacy, in any sense,
for the clinical benefits that we derive from

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:57.430
interoperability.

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:04.130
However, we recognise we live in a digital
world, where security cannot be guaranteed

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:06.560
all the time but obviously we need to meet
the highest standards.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:12.540
So the development of that new rubric around
what a safe health system looks like in a

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:17.199
digital age is one of the core priorities
for the National Information Board, as a collective,

00:12:17.199 --> 00:12:22.769
to solve and Dame Fiona Caldicott and others
are deeply engaged in working through what

00:12:22.769 --> 00:12:30.279
the principles of that kind of commitment,
that new contract, if I can put it like that,

00:12:30.279 --> 00:12:31.279
should be.

00:12:31.279 --> 00:12:32.279
Malcolm Grant: Thanks.

00:12:32.279 --> 00:12:33.279
Victor and then David?

00:12:33.279 --> 00:12:36.310
Victor Adebowale: I think we have to get on
with it, have we not, really?

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:42.959
It is overdue; we need to get on with it.

00:12:42.959 --> 00:12:53.149
However, some time ago I was curious about
patient citizen data and the possibility that

00:12:53.149 --> 00:12:59.791
individuals might receive some kind of report
on how their data is being used and you were

00:12:59.791 --> 00:13:01.190
quite keen on the idea, as I recall.

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:02.690
I just wondered what progress had been made?

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:08.660
I mean we just need to do this but I just
wondered about that particular bit.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:12.459
Tim Kelsey: We are committed to three bits
there.

00:13:12.459 --> 00:13:19.540
So the first thing is, very rapidly, certainly
during this next calendar year, people who

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:25.180
access their own GP records, as they now can
do, as of the end of this month, will also

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.940
be able to access a kind of an audit trail
of who else has seen their records because

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:32.980
that is something that is already collected;
I would say creating of visibility of that

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:38.100
to the patient is important and we can do
it, so, as you say, we are going to do it.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:42.230
More complex is the other commitment that
the National Information Board has made, to

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:45.759
giving some insight into how their data is
being used when it is being used for analytic

00:13:45.759 --> 00:13:52.709
purposes: you know, a researcher over here
or NHS England over there; who is using my

00:13:52.709 --> 00:13:53.709
data to what benefit?

00:13:53.709 --> 00:13:56.769
This would really just be to explain to people
how their data is contributing, let us say,

00:13:56.769 --> 00:14:00.879
to a better understanding of variation in
outcomes in diabetes; people want to know

00:14:00.879 --> 00:14:01.879
that.

00:14:01.879 --> 00:14:05.139
That is a more complex technical issue but
we are committed to resolving and to provide

00:14:05.139 --> 00:14:11.949
people with, as far as we can, full transparency
around the uses of their data for those purposes

00:14:11.949 --> 00:14:13.180
as well.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:20.399
The third thing is we want people to be able
to actually write into their own records so

00:14:20.399 --> 00:14:24.819
that there is a kind of parity between the
clinical voice and the patient voice in determining

00:14:24.819 --> 00:14:30.040
appropriate solutions for a person's health
and care journey, kind of almost like a personalised

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:35.089
care plan for the 21st Century; we have committed
to that taking place in 2018.

00:14:35.089 --> 00:14:40.790
I therefore think there is a radical commitment
to transparency of uses of data and of people

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:44.230
who are accessing your data, given it is your
data and you should have the right to decide

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:45.230
who sees it.

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:53.170
David Roberts: Tim, clearly this is take all
stakes for any form of modern health and care

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:58.329
system so, as Victor rightly said, we need
to get on with it.

00:14:58.329 --> 00:15:04.180
In terms of encouraging people to get on with,
there is one way, which is sort of the club,

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:06.029
which I think you are deploying.

00:15:06.029 --> 00:15:11.260
However, the other way is the motivational
aspects and actually the best way to do that,

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:14.380
I think, is to get the patients themselves
to demand it.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:19.279
I guess, therefore, my question would be:
are we doing enough, particularly in building

00:15:19.279 --> 00:15:26.040
apps, or enabling a market to build apps that
we are supportive of?

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:33.730
Are we being sufficiently aggressive in communicating
the benefits and showing people the benefits

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:38.699
of owning and seeing their own data in a way
that actually gives the providers who might

00:15:38.699 --> 00:15:45.699
be reticent no alternative because people
are just going to be unwilling to accept that

00:15:45.699 --> 00:15:49.189
they cannot get hold of their data and use
it?

00:15:49.189 --> 00:15:53.660
Tim Kelsey: It is a really big question but
the one amazing thing that has happened in

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:57.790
the last 12 months is that the community in
general practice, which did have the technical

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:02.370
facility to do this, has decided to do it.

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:09.900
So, although it is contractually required,
actually there has been an enormous recognition

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:13.439
of the potential benefits in general practice.

00:16:13.439 --> 00:16:17.589
So when your hospital colleagues or colleagues
in social care are looking, we have a very

00:16:17.589 --> 00:16:20.661
large constituency that is already saying,
'Well, we have just done it,' and I think

00:16:20.661 --> 00:16:25.259
that is a helpful narrative which we need
to make the most of and have not yet started

00:16:25.259 --> 00:16:26.259
to do.

00:16:26.259 --> 00:16:32.250
However, I could not agree more on, in a way,
activating the citizen to demand access to

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:37.040
these kinds of services, because they are
going to, so to try and make sure the NHS

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:41.509
is ready for that demand as it comes is a
very important objective.

00:16:41.509 --> 00:16:48.819
Indeed, this week we launched the first mental
health app store on NHS Choices, backed by

00:16:48.819 --> 00:16:53.550
the NHS, because we know that digital tools
work to drive up access to talking therapies

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:57.639
and people want it and the NHS just has not
expressed an opinion on whether these things

00:16:57.639 --> 00:16:58.639
work or not.

00:16:58.639 --> 00:17:03.440
David Roberts: This seems to be a strategic
theme for us because, again, in thinking about

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:12.600
how we can engage people in preventing illness
and condition, as opposed to 'suffering it',

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:20.500
in inverted commas, I think is a very key
part of our agenda going forward.

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:23.760
Tim Kelsey: Yes, I completely agree.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:24.760
Malcolm Grant: Moira?

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:27.370
Moira Gibb: Where I come back on all of this
is that I completely agree with the imperative

00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:29.810
to just get on with it.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:35.180
However, there is the back story which is
still in my mind and I think that what I am

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:42.851
asking is this: as you build your very large
and important, multi room building, every

00:17:42.851 --> 00:17:49.690
time you want to extend it, that we as a board
have an opportunity to look and see; check

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:54.210
that the building regulations requirements
have been met, if I can put it that way?

00:17:54.210 --> 00:18:00.590
We would need, therefore, periodic reports
to give us comfort about the systemisation

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:05.870
and how it is working; that would make me
feel comfortable and I will be quiet.

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:09.300
Tim Kelsey: No, I completely welcome that;
I think we have to be transparent in every

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:10.300
direction.

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:15.160
Certainly transparency to the Board of the
way in which this multi room - if that is

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:19.600
the right concept - this process is being
developed so that it is safe, it is clinically

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:23.430
compliant, it is actually benefiting people
in a really measurable way is really critical,

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:24.430
so I would certainly welcome that.

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:28.910
It almost goes back to your genomics point:
why would we do this rather than physiotherapists?

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:35.160
Malcolm Grant: Okay, look, I think we are
all clear: get on with it.

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:41.601
However, the issue is this is what Derek Wanless
wrote about in 2002, for goodness sake; the

00:18:41.601 --> 00:18:46.360
outcomes he held out for the NHS in 2002,
we still have not got there.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:52.510
I would now like to invite the board, on your
behalf, to approve the current priority list

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:58.650
of digital standards for 2015/16 and to support
the future priorities for 2016/18.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:04.560
We are happy to do that and to confirm our
intention to use all available commissioning

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:06.510
levers to get on with it.

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:13.380
Tim, thank you for this; I would not go so
far as to say this was a high, historic moment

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:21.190
but I do think that it is something of a turning
point and I hope that you feel you have the

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:23.170
full support of the board in taking it forward.

